// Probability tree whose two causes each split into the evidence and its complement.
//
// A two-argument size call bounds the output under Asymptote's default keepAspect
// and does not scale labels, so the compiler decides the box. This declaration is
// restated from a real compile by `npm run content:figures -- --record-aspect-ratios`.
size(420,380);
pen t = gray(0.55) + 1;
pen n = black + 1.4;
pair o = (0,0);
pair a = (3.6,1.7);
pair b = (3.6,-1.7);
dot(o, n);
draw(o--a, t);
draw(o--b, t);
label("start", o, W);
label("cause A", a, N);
label("cause B", b, S);
pair a1 = (7.4,2.9);
pair a2 = (7.4,0.5);
pair b1 = (7.4,-0.5);
pair b2 = (7.4,-2.9);
draw(a--a1, t);
draw(a--a2, t);
draw(b--b1, t);
draw(b--b2, t);
dot(a1, n);
label("E", a1, N);
dot(a2, n);
label("no E", a2, SE);
dot(b1, n);
label("E", b1, NW);
dot(b2, n);
label("no E", b2, SW);
label("P(A and E)", (5.4,2.2), NE);
label("P(B and E)", (5.4,-2.2), SE);

// Odds ladder: each rung multiplies the previous rung's odds by a likelihood ratio.
//
// A two-argument size call bounds the output under Asymptote's default keepAspect
// and does not scale labels, so the compiler decides the box. This declaration is
// restated from a real compile by `npm run content:figures -- --record-aspect-ratios`.
size(400,300);
pen t = gray(0.5) + 1;
pen n = black + 1.4;
pair c0 = (1.2,0);
pair c1 = (4.2,0);
pair c2 = (7.2,0);
dot(c0, n);
dot(c1, n);
dot(c2, n);
label("prior", c0, S);
label("one test", c1, S);
label("two tests", c2, S);
draw(c0--c1, t);
draw(c1--c2, t);
label("x 3", (2.7,0.5), N);
label("x 2", (5.7,0.5), N);
draw((1.2,1.3)--(1.2,1.9), n);
draw((4.2,1.3)--(4.2,1.9), n);
draw((7.2,1.3)--(7.2,1.9), n);
label("odds", (1.2,1.3), S);
label("odds", (4.2,1.3), S);
label("odds", (7.2,1.3), S);
label("likelihood ratios act on odds, never on probability", (4.2,-1.7), S);

items.bodyLatex:
Bayes' theorem is

P(C_i \mid E) = \frac{P(E \mid C_i)\, P(C_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^{k} P(E \mid C_j)\, P(C_j)}

The numerator pairs one cause with the evidence it produces and weights it by how common that cause was before the evidence arrived. Writing the numerator down first, as a prior times a likelihood, keeps the two halves of the problem from being swapped.

Dividing the theorem by its own complement gives the cleanest form:

\frac{P(C \mid E)}{P(C^c \mid E)} = \frac{P(C)}{P(C^c)} \cdot \frac{P(E \mid C)}{P(E \mid C^c)}

A positive test multiplies the prior odds by the likelihood ratio and touches nothing else. This is the form to reach for when the numbers are percentages, because it never requires computing $P(E)$ at all.
